Chapter: DiagPci
  cites Foreword chapter MemoryMap
  cites Kernel chapter Pci
  cites Diag chapter DiagStage

 A chipset-integrated part (the PCH's own Ethernet, SATA and xHCI) sits on
 bus 0. A discrete one sits behind a PCI-to-PCI bridge, on a bus whose
 number only that bridge knows, so bus 0 alone answers the question on
 some boards and silently answers nothing on others.

 We say:

Section: Hex

  pp-nib : Integer -> Text
  pp-nib (n) =
    if n == 0 then "0" else if n == 1 then "1" else if n == 2 then "2"
    else if n == 3 then "3" else if n == 4 then "4" else if n == 5 then "5"
    else if n == 6 then "6" else if n == 7 then "7" else if n == 8 then "8"
    else if n == 9 then "9" else if n == 10 then "a" else if n == 11 then "b"
    else if n == 12 then "c" else if n == 13 then "d" else if n == 14 then "e"
    else "f"

  pp-hx : Integer, Integer -> Text
  pp-hx (v) (sh) = pp-nib (bit-and (bit-shru v sh) 15)

  pp-hex2 : Integer -> Text
  pp-hex2 (v) = pp-hx v 4 & pp-hx v 0

  pp-hex4 : Integer -> Text
  pp-hex4 (v) = pp-hx v 12 & pp-hx v 8 & pp-hx v 4 & pp-hx v 0

  pp-hex8 : Integer -> Text
  pp-hex8 (v) = pp-hex4 (bit-shru v 16) & pp-hex4 v

Section: Config Reads

  pp-cfg : PciDevice, Integer -> Integer
  pp-cfg (d) (off) = pci-read-config (d.pci-bus) (d.pci-dev) (d.pci-func) off

 Register 8 carries the revision in its low byte and the programming
 interface in the next one up; the class and subclass above them are
 already on the record. Prog-if is what separates an AHCI controller from
 the same silicon in IDE mode, and xHCI (48) from EHCI (32).

  pp-progif : PciDevice -> Integer
  pp-progif (d) = bit-and (bit-shru (pp-cfg d 8) 8) 255

  pp-rev : PciDevice -> Integer
  pp-rev (d) = bit-and (pp-cfg d 8) 255

 Register 24 on a bridge holds the primary bus in its low byte and the
 SECONDARY bus in the next one: the number of the bus on the far side,
 which is the one to walk.

  pp-sec-bus : PciDevice -> Integer
  pp-sec-bus (d) = bit-and (bit-shru (pp-cfg d 24) 8) 255

Section: The Bus Walk

  pp-collect : Integer, Integer, List PciDevice -> List PciDevice
  pp-collect (bus) (depth) (acc) =
    if depth > 3 then acc
    else let s = pci-scan-bus bus
    in pp-bridges (s.devices) (s.count) 0 bus depth (acc & s.devices)

  pp-bridges : List PciDevice, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List PciDevice -> List PciDevice
  pp-bridges (devs) (n) (i) (bus) (depth) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else if (list-at devs i).pci-header-type == 1 then pp-bridge-one (list-at devs i) devs n i bus depth acc
    else pp-bridges devs n (i + 1) bus depth acc

 A secondary bus at or below the bus being walked is either an unconfigured
 bridge or a malformed one, and following it would not terminate. The depth
 cap above is the second guard on the same hazard.

  pp-bridge-one : PciDevice, List PciDevice, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List PciDevice -> List PciDevice
  pp-bridge-one (d) (devs) (n) (i) (bus) (depth) (acc) =
    let sec = pp-sec-bus d
    in if sec > bus then pp-bridges devs n (i + 1) bus depth (pp-collect sec (depth + 1) acc)
    else pp-bridges devs n (i + 1) bus depth acc

Section: Classification

  pp-is-net : PciDevice -> Boolean
  pp-is-net (d) = d.pci-class == 2

  pp-is-storage : PciDevice -> Boolean
  pp-is-storage (d) = d.pci-class == 1

  pp-is-usb : PciDevice -> Boolean
  pp-is-usb (d) = if d.pci-class == 12 then d.pci-subclass == 3 else False

  pp-kind-of : PciDevice -> Integer
  pp-kind-of (d) =
    if pp-is-net d then 2
    else if pp-is-storage d then 1
    else if pp-is-usb d then 12
    else 0

  pp-colour : PciDevice -> Integer
  pp-colour (d) =
    if pp-is-net d then 16776960
    else if pp-is-storage d then 65535
    else if pp-is-usb d then 65280
    else 9474192

  pp-find : List PciDevice, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  pp-find (devs) (n) (i) (kind) =
    if i >= n then -1
    else if pp-kind-of (list-at devs i) == kind then i
    else pp-find devs n (i + 1) kind

Section: Formatting

  pp-bdf : PciDevice -> Text
  pp-bdf (d) = pp-hex2 (d.pci-bus) & ":" & pp-hex2 (d.pci-dev) & "." & show (d.pci-func)

  pp-ids : PciDevice -> Text
  pp-ids (d) = pp-hex4 (d.pci-vendor) & ":" & pp-hex4 (d.pci-device-id)

  pp-cls : PciDevice -> Text
  pp-cls (d) = pp-hex2 (d.pci-class) & "." & pp-hex2 (d.pci-subclass) & "." & pp-hex2 (pp-progif d)

  pp-line : PciDevice -> Text
  pp-line (d) = pp-bdf d & " " & pp-ids d & " " & pp-cls d

 The subsystem pair is what names the board variant of a part whose
 vendor:device is shared across a whole family, and the BARs are raw
 rather than parsed: a one-shot reading should carry what the register
 held, not this chapter's reading of it.

  pp-tail-a : PciDevice -> Text
  pp-tail-a (d) =
    let sub = pp-cfg d 44
    in " r" & pp-hex2 (pp-rev d) & " s" & pp-hex4 (bit-and sub 65535) & ":" & pp-hex4 (bit-shru sub 16)

 BAR0 is where a NIC and an xHCI put their register window, and BAR5 is
 where an AHCI controller puts its: on an ICH9 in AHCI mode BAR0 through
 BAR4 are the vestigial IDE bars and read back zero, so a reading that
 took BAR0 alone would report a storage controller at address nothing.
 BAR1 is carried because it holds the high half of a 64-bit BAR0.

  pp-tail-b : PciDevice -> Text
  pp-tail-b (d) =
    " i" & pp-hex2 (bit-and (pp-cfg d 60) 255) & " B0=" & pp-hex8 (pp-cfg d 16) & " B1=" & pp-hex8 (pp-cfg d 20) & " B5=" & pp-hex8 (pp-cfg d 36)

 Whether the register window is REACHABLE, which is a different question
 from where it is. The runtime page tables map exactly one gigabyte above
 RAM for devices, 3 GB to 4 GB. A BAR the firmware placed in that range
 can be read as soon as the device is enabled; a 64-bit BAR placed above
 4 GB is not mapped at all, and a driver would need another page
 directory before it could read a single register. Both are ordinary
 outcomes of firmware assignment, so the probe answers it rather than
 leaving the arithmetic to be done at the machine.

 A BAR whose bit 0 is set is an I/O-space BAR (PCI 3.0 section 6.2.5.1):
 its upper bits are a port number, not an address, so it says nothing
 about memory reachability. The RTL8168 on the ASUS puts I/O in BAR0 and
 its MMIO in the 64-bit BAR2, and judging BAR0 there read a port as an
 address below 3 GB (HardwareSitting.md, diag.img flight 2026-08-18). A
 memory BAR with bits 2..1 equal to 2 is 64-bit and its high dword is
 the next BAR; an AHCI controller in AHCI mode reads zero for BAR0
 through BAR4 and carries its window in BAR5.

  pp-bar-words : PciDevice, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  pp-bar-words (d) (off) (acc) =
    if off > 36 then acc
    else pp-bar-words d (off + 4) (list-push acc (pp-cfg d off))

  pp-bar-mem-base : List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  pp-bar-mem-base (bars) (i) =
    if i >= list-length bars then 0
    else let lo = list-at bars i
    in if lo == 0 then pp-bar-mem-base bars (i + 1)
    else if bit-and lo 1 == 1 then pp-bar-mem-base bars (i + 1)
    else if bit-and (bit-shru lo 1) 3 == 2 then bit-or (bit-and lo 4294967280) (bit-shl (bit-and (if i + 1 < list-length bars then list-at bars (i + 1) else 0) 4294967295) 32)
    else bit-and lo 4294967280

  pp-map-none : Integer = 0
  pp-map-ok : Integer = 1
  pp-map-above4g : Integer = 2
  pp-map-below3g : Integer = 3

  pp-map-judge : List Integer -> Integer
  pp-map-judge (bars) =
    let b = pp-bar-mem-base bars 0
    in if b == 0 then pp-map-none
    else if b < bare-metal-device-window-lo then pp-map-below3g
    else if b < bare-metal-device-window-hi then pp-map-ok
    else pp-map-above4g

  pp-map-of : PciDevice -> Integer
  pp-map-of (d) = pp-map-judge (pp-bar-words d 16 [])

  pp-map-word : Integer -> Text
  pp-map-word (m) =
    if m == pp-map-ok then "ok"
    else if m == pp-map-below3g then "BELOW3G"
    else if m == pp-map-above4g then "ABOVE4G"
    else "none"

  pp-map-note : PciDevice -> Text
  pp-map-note (d) = " MAP=" & pp-map-word (pp-map-of d)

  pp-tail : PciDevice -> Text
  pp-tail (d) = pp-tail-a d & pp-tail-b d & pp-map-note d

Section: The Body

  pp-body : List PciDevice, Integer -> Text
  pp-body (devs) (n) = "PCIPROBE1 n=" & show n & ";" & pp-body-loop devs n 0 ""

  pp-body-loop : List PciDevice, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  pp-body-loop (devs) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else let d = list-at devs i
    in let one = pp-line d & (if pp-kind-of d == 0 then "" else pp-tail d) & ";"
    in pp-body-loop devs n (i + 1) (acc & one)

Section: The Stage

  dpci-states : List Text = ["ok", "unassigned", "ABOVE4G", "BELOW3G", "empty"]

 The stage state is the worst BAR verdict among the parts a driver would
 bind, in the order the README ranks them: BELOW3G is mapped as RAM inside
 the arena and fails somewhere else entirely, ABOVE4G faults loudly on the
 first register read, and only then does an unassigned BAR count.

  pp-worst-map : List PciDevice, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  pp-worst-map (devs) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else let d = list-at devs i
    in if pp-kind-of d == 0 then pp-worst-map devs n (i + 1) acc
    else let m = pp-map-of d
    in let acc2 = if m == pp-map-below3g then 3 else if m == pp-map-above4g then (if acc == 3 then 3 else 2) else if m == pp-map-none then (if acc >= 2 then acc else 1) else acc
    in pp-worst-map devs n (i + 1) acc2

  pp-stage-state : List PciDevice, Integer -> Text
  pp-stage-state (devs) (n) =
    if n == 0 then "empty"
    else let w = pp-worst-map devs n 0 0
    in if w == 3 then "BELOW3G"
    else if w == 2 then "ABOVE4G"
    else if w == 1 then "unassigned"
    else "ok"

  pp-stage-colour : Text -> Integer
  pp-stage-colour (s) =
    if s == "ok" then diag-col-good
    else if s == "BELOW3G" then diag-col-bad
    else if s == "empty" then diag-col-bad
    else diag-col-warn

  pp-callout : List PciDevice, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  pp-callout (devs) (n) (kind) (label) =
    let i = pp-find devs n 0 kind
    in if i < 0 then label & "=none"
    else let d = list-at devs i
    in label & "=" & pp-bdf d & " " & pp-ids d & " MAP=" & pp-map-word (pp-map-of d)

  pp-bank-lines : List PciDevice, Integer, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  pp-bank-lines (devs) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else let d = list-at devs i
    in pp-bank-lines devs n (i + 1) (list-push acc ("pci " & pp-line d & (if pp-kind-of d == 0 then "" else pp-tail d)))

  dpci-run : DiagCtx -> DiagResult
  dpci-run (c) =
    let scan = c.dc-pci
    in let devs = scan.devices
    in let n = scan.count
    in let state = pp-stage-state devs n
    in let g1 = "devices=" & show n & " " & pp-callout devs n 2 "nic"
    in let g2 = pp-callout devs n 1 "storage" & " " & pp-callout devs n 12 "usb"
    in let bank = pp-bank-lines devs n 0 [g1, g2]
    in diag-result state (pp-stage-colour state) [g1, g2] bank
